    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204768": {
      "title": "Assistant Club Coach, Ice Hockey",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Club Coach, Ice Hockey Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Club Coach, Ice Hockey Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Club Coach, Ice Hockey Category: Athletics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Athletics Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Temporary Ref. No.: 1451 Position ID: 204768 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Under the direction of the Director of Athletics, Intramurals and Recreation, the Assistant Club Coach, Men's Ice Hockey is responsible for all aspects of the development and administration of a successful Intercollegiate Men's Ice Hockey Club Sport Program. The program will be conducted in accordance with all University and American Collegiate Hockey Association (ACHA) rules, regulations, and policies. Coaching/Teaching Duties Teach and develop sport-specific skills in a successful, safe and prudent manner. Create, present, and maintain records of up-to-date lesson plans for pre-season/season practice drills and procedures detailing the desired goals to be achieved. Create a schedule of practice and game sessions, and conduct post game analysis and incorporate into practice plans. Administrative Duties Conduct an ongoing recruitment program through a variety of methods, both on and off the ice with the goal to attract, select and encourage enrollment of high caliber student-athletes. Maintain records of up-to-date equipment inventory for future ordering. Initiate new ideas and methods to improve the program in support of the University goal of a successful conference record. Additional Functions Fundraise for assigned program and coordinate and support fundraising efforts. Maintain the highest level of confidentiality and uses discretion when supporting students, their families, and working with sensitive student information. Requirements: Bachelor's degree, preferably in a sports related discipline. Minimum two years' experience in sports coaching and knowledge of the fundamentals, tactics, and strategies of intercollegiate sports with the ability to recruit talented players for the program is preferred. Excellent oral and written communication skills.  Ability to function in a service and team-oriented environment. Ability to function independently, display strong attention to detail, work under pressure, meet deadlines and prioritize multiple projects. Excellent interpersonal skills, ability to communicate and work collaboratively with a variety of individuals, University community including faculty, students, staff, parents and alumni. Ability to advise and motivate student-athletes. Must be able to travel as required. Possess a valid driver's license. Available to work a flexible schedule including nights, weekends, and some holidays. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Department: Athletics Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204744": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Diversity Office Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 11, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2839 Position ID: 204744 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Looking to start or make a move into a career in higher education? If you enjoy working with college students and care about student engagement, mentoring, intercultural learning, leadership, and student success, this may be the role for you. We welcome new professionals, career changers, and applicants from a range of professional and educational backgrounds. If this work interests you and you meet the required qualifications, we encourage you to apply. The Assistant Director, Intercultural Student Success and Engagement is assigned primarily to the Intercultural Center (IC) and reports to the Director of Institutional Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion. The role coordinates student support, academic coaching, mentoring, student leadership development, intercultural education, student success initiatives, and community-building programs for students. The Assistant Director supports students who engage with the IC, with particular expertise in the experiences of first-generation students and students from varied cultural, linguistic, religious, socioeconomic, and educational backgrounds. The role uses an intersectional approach to strengthen access, belonging, retention, persistence, well-being, leadership, and academic success. The Assistant Director supports prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students and contributes to selected alumni-engagement activities. Responsibilities include academic coaching, mentoring, student organization advising, peer mentoring, resource navigation, intercultural programming and education, assessment, communications, and campus partnerships. The role also supports education and campus partnerships related to intercultural dialogue, bias awareness, inclusive student engagement, and broader Division of Equity and Inclusion priorities focused on a thriving campus community. Student Support, Engagement, and Resource Navigation Provide academic coaching, mentoring, referral, and follow-up for students who seek support through the IC, with attention to the educational, financial, cultural, linguistic, and family circumstances affecting student success. Support student access, retention, persistence, belonging, and success through proactive outreach, resource navigation, academic success planning, and timely connection to campus partners. Partner with advising, tutoring, counseling, Residence Life and Housing, accessibility, health services, student conduct, career education, financial aid, faculty, admissions, and other offices to reduce barriers and coordinate student support. Track student contacts, referrals, follow-up themes, academic support needs, and student success trends for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Support prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students through programs and short-term projects tied to access, persistence, retention, and success, and contribute to selected alumni-engagement activities. Intercultural Center Programs, Services, and Events Develop, maintain, implement, and evaluate IC services, programs, events, workshops, and student engagement initiatives. Coordinate programs grounded in intercultural learning, identity development, leadership, dialogue, belonging, and community building. Support inclusive signature programs, heritage and cultural education, recognition efforts, welcome programs, graduation celebrations, academic enhancement workshops, community meals, dialogue programs, and first-generation student initiatives. Develop and support intercultural dialogue, bias awareness, inclusive practice, and community-building education for students, student leaders, campus partners, and assigned groups. Create measurable program goals, learning outcomes, timelines, logistics plans, assessment tools, and post-program reports. Use student feedback and participation data to recommend improvements to IC programs and services. Student Organizations, Leadership Development, and Campus Partnerships Advise assigned recognized student organizations and student leaders connected to the IC, including organizations focused on culture, religion, first-generation student experiences, community engagement, and intercultural learning. Maintain regular engagement with assigned student organizations and student leaders through meetings with organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, area coordinators, and relevant campus partners. Attend selected evening and weekend events as needed. Provide mentorship, continuity, conflict navigation, and leadership coaching for student organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, peer leaders, and emerging student leaders. Support student leaders with program planning, budget management, university policies, risk management, and organizational sustainability. Assist student groups with funding processes, room reservations, purchasing, event preparation, administrative steps, and campus liaison work. Partner with Residence Life and Housing to support approved residential communities through student-leader development, education, programming, community meetings, and referrals. Residence Life and Housing retains responsibility for eligibility, selection, placement, and housing policy. Division Support, Campus Partnerships, and In-Person and Virtual Yield Events Support the work of the Division of Equity and Inclusion, including assigned projects that advance broader divisional priorities. Attend regular one-on-one supervisory meetings with the Director, Center meetings, Division meetings, and other assigned campus meetings. Serve as an active participant on assigned committees, working groups, and campus partnerships related to student access, persistence, retention, belonging, safety, well-being, and success. Participate in admissions and yield events, new student orientation, welcome and move-in programs, open houses, admitted-student events, pre-college programs, alumni events, and outreach activities on and off campus. Partner with alumni, faculty, staff, students, and community partners on short-term projects that support student access, persistence, retention, and success. Assessment, Data, Communications, and Reporting Collect and report appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information consistent with FERPA, university privacy and data-governance requirements, and principles of data minimization. Sensitive student information will be collected only when necessary, maintained with appropriate access controls, and reported in aggregate or deidentified form when feasible. Maintain accurate records related to student contacts, referrals, programs, trainings, spending, attendance, assessment results, student support trends, and annual reporting. Track referrals, follow-up needs, participation, program outcomes, and student support themes for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Prepare concise reports, summaries, and recommendations based on program outcomes, student feedback, assessment findings, and student support trends. Use appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information to identify barriers, assess effectiveness, document impact, and improve IC programs and services. Assist with social media, website updates, newsletters, marketing materials, and other web-based communications for the IC and the Division. Operations, Budget, Safety, and Administration Help manage daily IC operations, including space use, student-facing resources, supplies, equipment, scheduling, calendars, files, and general office workflow. Assist in the supervision and development of undergraduate and graduate student staff and volunteers. Track and report assigned spending in alignment with university policies and purchasing requirements. Report concerns related to safety, facilities, maintenance, accessibility, technology, and operations. Support a welcoming, organized, accessible, and student-centered IC environment. Participate in required training and ongoing professional development. Requirements: Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree. A minimum of 2 to 3 years relevant experience with academic coaching, mentoring, student support, education, or program development, including experience gained through professional roles, graduate assistantships, internships, student leadership, peer education, residence life and housing, new student orientation, admissions, student activities, resource navigation, or related work. Relevant experience includes, but is not limited to, supporting student access, belonging, retention, persistence, and success across varied cultural, linguistic, religious, socioeconomic, and educational backgrounds, including first-generation and multilingual student experiences, with attention to intersectionality. Strong written and oral communication skills across various audiences. Experience using Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, Excel, and related tools. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in higher education, student affairs, education, counseling, social work, sociology, social justice, ethnic studies, gender and sexuality studies, multicultural education, public health, communications, business, or a related field. Professional experience in a higher education, nonprofit, education, community-based, or student support setting related to intercultural student services, multicultural affairs, student support, student success, access, retention, or first-generation student support. Experience supporting student success or access-focused programs, such as first-generation student programs, recognized student organizations, heritage and cultural programming, student recognition programs, residential-community programming, or pre-college access programs. Experience developing or facilitating programs, workshops, trainings, or student engagement initiatives related to intercultural learning, identity development, belonging, inclusive practice, study skills, student leadership, or community building. Knowledge in one or more of the following areas: student development theory, racial identity development, intercultural learning, belonging research, intersectionality, trauma-informed practice, and campus policies and practices affecting diverse student populations, including student conduct, bias response, privacy, and campus safety. Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data to assess student needs, program participation, learning outcomes, student engagement, or program effectiveness. Experience supervising undergraduate students, graduate students, interns, peer educators, or volunteers. Experience with website maintenance, social media, digital communications, marketing, or outreach. Bilingual or multilingual ability, or experience working with multilingual students and families. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Applicants should submit the following materials: Resume or curriculum vitae. Cover letter describing relevant experience in three or more of the following areas: academic coaching, mentoring, program development, training and education, student success, student support, leadership development, and assessment. Three professional references. For each reference, include the person's first and last name, professional title, organization, phone number, and email address. This position will remain open until filled. Review of applications will begin on Monday, October 5, 2026, and continue on a rolling basis. Candidates are encouraged to apply as soon as possible. Department: Diversity Office Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204743": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement Category: Professional and Clerical Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Diversity Office Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 11, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 2838 Position ID: 204743 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Looking to start or make a move into a career in higher education? If you enjoy working with college students and care about student engagement, mentoring, LGBTQIA+ education, leadership, and student success, this may be the role for you. We welcome new professionals, career changers, and applicants from a range of professional and educational backgrounds. If this work interests you and you meet the required qualifications, we encourage you to apply. The Assistant Director, Queer and Trans Student Success and Engagement is assigned primarily to the Queer and Trans Resource and Advocacy Center (QTRAC) and reports to the Director of Institutional Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion. The role coordinates student support, academic coaching, mentoring, student leadership development, LGBTQIA+ education, and community-building programs for students. The Assistant Director serves all students including queer, trans, nonbinary, gender nonconforming, questioning, asexual, and intersex students, as well as allied students. The role uses an intersectional approach to strengthen access, belonging, retention, persistence, well-being, leadership, and full participation in university life. The Assistant Director supports prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students and contributes to selected alumni-engagement activities. Responsibilities include academic coaching, mentoring, student organization advising, peer mentoring, programming, training, assessment, communications, and campus partnerships. The role provides education, student support, and resource navigation related to gender identity, sexual orientation, chosen-name and pronoun processes, facilities, housing, and other student-facing systems. The Assistant Director works in coordination with the supervisor and the university offices responsible for policy, compliance, housing, facilities, and student records. The role also supports broader Division of Equity and Inclusion priorities focused on a thriving campus community. Student Support, Engagement, and Resource Navigation Provide academic coaching, mentoring, referral, and follow-up for students including queer, trans, nonbinary, gender nonconforming, questioning, asexual, intersex, and allied students, with particular attention to first-generation students and the ways intersectionality shapes queer and trans student experiences. Support student access, retention, persistence, belonging, identity development, leadership, and full participation in university life. Assist students with resource navigation through established university processes, referrals, and partnerships with responsible offices related to wellness, family concerns, bias experiences, academic success, and campus participation. Partner with advising, tutoring, counseling, Residence Life and Housing, accessibility, health services, Title IX, student conduct, public safety, career education, financial aid, faculty, admissions, and other offices to reduce barriers and coordinate student support. Track student contacts, referrals, support themes, climate concerns, and student success trends for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Support prospective, pre-college, and enrolled students through programs and short-term projects tied to access, persistence, retention, and success, and contribute to selected alumni-engagement activities. QTRAC Programs, Services, and Events Develop, maintain, implement, and evaluate QTRAC services, programs, events, workshops, and student engagement initiatives. Coordinate LGBTQIA+ programs grounded in queer and trans identity development, wellness, leadership, belonging, community building, and joy. Coordinate signature programs and visibility events such as Lavender Graduation, Coming Out Day, Trans Week of Remembrance, Trans Day of Visibility, Pride Week, queer and trans wellness programs, and community-building events. Create measurable program goals, learning outcomes, timelines, logistics plans, assessment tools, and post-program reports. Use student feedback and participation data to recommend improvements to QTRAC programs and services. Training, Education, and Campus Partnerships Develop and deliver education on gender identity, sexual orientation, allyship, pronouns, chosen names, facilities, queer and trans student support, and student-facing systems using approved university information and practices. Provide education and resource guidance to campus partners, in collaboration with the supervisor and responsible university offices, to support queer and trans student engagement and success. Refer questions involving policy interpretation, compliance, student records, housing, facilities, or institutional procedures to the responsible university office. Track training registration, attendance, learning outcomes, feedback, curriculum updates, and follow-up needs. Identify and share approved practices related to queer and trans student support with campus partners and Division leadership. Student Organizations, Leadership Development, and Campus Partnerships Advise assigned recognized student organizations and student leaders connected to QTRAC, including LGBTQIA+, queer and trans, gender justice, and allied student organizations (e.g., SAGA, Feminists United, and the Kinship Collaborative). Maintain regular engagement with assigned student organizations and student leaders through meetings with organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, area coordinators, and relevant campus partners. Attend selected evening and weekend events as needed. Provide mentorship, continuity, conflict navigation, and leadership coaching for student organization presidents, executive boards, resident assistants, peer leaders, and emerging student leaders. Support student leaders with program planning, budget management, university policies, risk management, and organizational sustainability. Assist student groups with funding processes, room reservations, purchasing, event preparation, administrative steps, and campus liaison work. Partner with Residence Life and Housing to support approved residential communities through student-leader development, education, programming, community meetings, and referrals. Residence Life and Housing retains responsibility for eligibility, selection, placement, and housing policy. Division Support, Campus Partnerships, and In-Person and Virtual Yield Events Support the work of the Division of Equity and Inclusion, including assigned projects that advance broader divisional priorities. Attend regular one-on-one supervisory meetings with the Director, Center meetings, Division meetings, and other assigned campus meetings. Serve as an active participant on assigned committees, working groups, and campus partnerships related to queer and trans student access, persistence, retention, belonging, safety, well-being, and success. Participate in admissions and yield events, new student orientation, welcome and move-in programs, open houses, admitted-student events, pre-college programs, alumni events, and outreach activities on and off campus. Partner with alumni, faculty, staff, students, and community partners on short-term projects that support queer and trans student access, persistence, retention, and success. Assessment, Data, Communications, and Reporting Collect and report appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information consistent with FERPA, university privacy and data-governance requirements, and principles of data minimization. Sensitive student information will be collected only when necessary, maintained with appropriate access controls, and reported in aggregate or deidentified form when feasible. Maintain accurate records related to student contacts, referrals, programs, trainings, spending, attendance, assessment results, student support trends, and annual reporting. Track referrals, follow-up needs, participation, program outcomes, training outcomes, and student support themes for review with the supervisor and relevant partners. Prepare concise reports, summaries, and recommendations based on program outcomes, student feedback, assessment findings, and student support trends. Use appropriate program, participation, referral, and outcome information to identify barriers, assess effectiveness, document impact, and improve QTRAC programs and services. Assist with social media, website updates, newsletters, marketing materials, and other web-based communications for QTRAC and the Division. Operations, Budget, Safety, and Administration Help manage daily QTRAC operations, including space use, student-facing resources, supplies, equipment, scheduling, calendars, files, and general office workflow. Assist in the supervision and development of undergraduate and graduate student staff and volunteers. Track and report assigned spending in alignment with university policies and purchasing requirements. Report concerns related to safety, facilities, maintenance, accessibility, technology, and operations. Support a welcoming, organized, accessible, and student-centered QTRAC environment. Participate in required training and ongoing professional development. Requirements: Bachelor's degree. A minimum of 2 to 3 years relevant experience with academic coaching, mentoring, student support, education, or program development, including experience gained through professional roles, graduate assistantships, internships, student leadership, peer education, residence life and housing, new student orientation, admissions, student activities, resource navigation, or related work. Relevant experience includes, but is not limited to, supporting access, belonging, retention, persistence, and success for queer, trans, nonbinary, gender nonconforming, questioning, asexual, intersex, and allied students, including first-generation students and students whose experiences are shaped by intersectionality. Experience developing, implementing, or facilitating programs, events, workshops, trainings, or student engagement initiatives related to gender identity, sexual orientation, allyship, student support, inclusive practice, social justice education, or queer and trans student engagement. Strong written and oral communication skills across various audiences. Experience using Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, Excel, and related tools. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in higher education, student affairs, education, counseling, social work, sociology, social justice, ethnic studies, gender and sexuality studies, multicultural education, public health, communications, business, or a related field. Professional experience in a higher education, nonprofit, education, community-based, or student support setting related to LGBTQIA+ student services, queer and trans student support, gender and sexuality programs, student engagement, inclusive practice, or queer and trans community building. Experience supporting queer and trans student initiatives, such as Safe Zone education, Lavender Graduation, queer and trans peer mentoring, trans student support, referrals and partnerships with responsible offices, resource navigation through established university processes, or LGBTQIA+ student organizations. Experience designing, facilitating, and assessing recurring education or training programs for students, faculty, staff, or campus partners. Knowledge in one or more of the following areas: student development theory, queer and trans identity development, sexual orientation and gender identity development, intersectionality, trauma-informed practice, and campus policies and practices affecting LGBTQIA+ students, including Title IX, student conduct, bias response, privacy, and campus safety. Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data to assess student needs, program participation, learning outcomes, student engagement, or program effectiveness. Experience supervising undergraduate students, graduate students, interns, peer educators, or volunteers. Experience with website maintenance, social media, digital communications, marketing, or outreach. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Applicants should submit the following materials: Resume or curriculum vitae. Cover letter describing relevant experience in three or more of the following areas: academic coaching, mentoring, program development, training and education, student success, student support, leadership development, and assessment. Three professional references. For each reference, include the person's first and last name, professional title, organization, phone number, and email address. This position will remain open until filled. Review of applications will begin on Monday, October 5, 2026, and continue on a rolling basis. Candidates are encouraged to apply as soon as possible. Department: Diversity Office Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204818": {
      "title": "Assistant Coach, Women's Soccer",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Assistant Coach, Women's Soccer Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Coach, Women's Soccer Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Coach, Women's Soccer Category: Athletics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Athletics Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 15, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Temporary Ref. No.: 1465 Position ID: 204818 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Under the direction of the Head Coach, the Assistant Coach is responsible for all aspects of the development and administration of a successful NCAA Division III Intercollegiate Sport Program. The program will be conducted in accordance with all University and National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA) rules, regulations, and policies. The Assistant Coach is a professional employee of the University with a primary duty of teaching and instructing student-athletes in the specific sport that is being coached. The vast majority of the Assistant Coach's time is spent on coaching/teaching duties, while a minority of the Assistant Coach's time is spent performing administrative duties. Coaching/Teaching Teach and develop sport-specific skills in a successful, safe and prudent manner. Create, present, and maintain records of up-to-date lesson plans for pre-season/season practice drills and procedures detailing the desired goals to be achieved. Schedule practice and game preparation sessions, and conduct post game analysis and incorporate into lesson plans. Conduct research of opposing teams. Administrative Conduct an ongoing recruitment program through a variety of methods, both on and off the court/field with the goal to attract, select and encourage enrollment of high caliber student-athletes. Initiate new ideas and methods to improve the program in support of the University goal of a successful conference and attainment of a regional level Intercollegiate Associate ranking relevant to the specific sport. Assist the Head Coach with the development of sport specific skills, in a successful, safe and prudent manner. Additional Functions Comply with the sport specific intercollegiate affiliation policy, conference rules, and department policy and procedures. Requirements: Bachelor's degree (B.A. or B.S.) in a related discipline from an accredited four-year collegiate institution. Experience in couching and knowledge of the fundamentals, tactics, and strategies of coaching intercollegiate sports with ability to recruit talented players for the program. Knowledgeable and current on all College rules and regulations concerning eligibility and recruiting related to specific sport that is being coached. Ability to handle emergency situations involving students. Current CPR and First Aid certification. Current driver's license. Ability to travel. Preferred Qualifications Intercollegiate sport specific experience. Ability to work on nights, weekends and some holidays. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Department: Athletics Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
    },
    "https://rwu.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204861": {
      "title": "Refrigeration/Appliance/HVAC Technician I, Facilities Management",
      "description": "Roger Williams University\nJob Posting: Refrigeration/Appliance/HVAC Technician I, Facilities Management Roger Williams University \n\tJob Posting: Refrigeration/Appliance/HVAC Technician I, Facilities Management Title Home Employment Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Refrigeration/Appliance/HVAC Technician I, Facilities Management Category: Facilities Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Facilities Management Locations: Bristol, RI Posted: Sep 16, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Regular Ref. No.: 1102 Position ID: 204861 Share About Roger Williams University: At Roger Williams University, students are prepared to be thinkers and doers ready to solve challenging problems with innovative solutions. RWU offers 50 majors and robust offerings of graduate and professional programs across eight schools of study including Rhode Island's only law school, with campuses on the coast of Bristol and in the heart of Providence, R.I. With small classrooms and a focus on undergraduate and graduate research and community engagement, a Roger Williams education blends the strength of liberal arts and professional studies, providing all students with the depth and breadth of interdisciplinary thinking and well-rounded knowledge necessary to succeed in our interconnected, global world. RWU is committed to strengthening society through engaged scholarship so that students graduate with the ability to think critically and apply the practical skills that today's employers demand. Roger Williams University seeks to recruit and support a broadly diverse community of faculty and staff. We value all races, genders, identities, and abilities. RWU is committed to creating a thriving community that encourages lifelong learning, professional development, and academic innovation. To learn more about Roger Williams University's commitment and support of diversity and inclusion, please visit https://www.rwu.edu/who-we-are/diversity-equity-inclusion . Job Description: Analyze, repair and maintain ventilation and air conditioning systems. Equipment Evacuate and recharge system. Remove freon from system by means of a recovery machine. HVAC When required will work with the Energy Management Technicians on resolving HVAC related issues. Installations Installs, inspects, operates and repairs appliances and HVAC equipment using necessary hand tools and testing equipment. Repairs Repairs freon leaks and retest for freon leaks. Upgrades Assist the Manager of Mechanical and Electrical Systems and the Mechanical, Electrical, Plumbing Systems Supervisor in projects to upgrade and modify existing building HVAC systems. Requirements: Technical school training and minimum 5 years' experience. Must be licensed to purchase and recover refrigerant in the State of Rhode Island. Must hold current RI refrigeration and/or RI pipe fitter Journeyman license. Must have 5 years' experience in a corporate/commercial type setting, maintaining HVAC systems. Must be extremely proficient in interpreting and understanding building drawings and documentation. Perform any and all tasks required of the position without assistance and in a timely manner. Must also demonstrate to the reasonable satisfaction of management, the ability to provide expert guidance, direction and remediation to levels III and/or II Refrigeration/Appliance/HVAC Technicians. At the University's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be accepted where the candidate can demonstrate, to the satisfaction of the University, an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position. Additional Information: Roger Williams University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and committed to a diverse workforce. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, or any other basis protected by applicable state and federal law. For information on our Non-discrimination policy, visit here . RWU prohibits all forms of discrimination on the basis of sex in its education programs and activities and is required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972 (Title IX) and its implementing regulations not to discriminate in such a manner. Complaints of Title IX sexual harassment should be reported to the Title IX Coordinator at the contact information listed below. Details regarding the formal complaint process can be found here: Title IX Sexual Harassment Policy and Procedures . All complaints of sexual harassment will be taken seriously and responded to promptly. TITLE IX COORDINATOR : Dr. Jen Stanley, 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809 / Phone Number: (401) 254-3123 /Email Address: jstanley@rwu.edu Application Instructions: Please attach a resume, cover letter, and list of three (3) professional references. Department: Facilities Management Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 1 Old Ferry Road, Bristol, RI 02809"
